  "detailed_explanation": {
    "concept": "Lagrange multipliers provide a method for finding the extrema of a function subject to constraints. The method introduces a new variable (the Lagrange multiplier) that allows us to incorporate the constraint into the optimization problem.",
    "mathematical_formulation": "For a function f(x,y) subject to constraint g(x,y) = c, we form the Lagrangian L(x,y,λ) = f(x,y) - λ(g(x,y) - c). The critical points are found by solving ∇L = 0, which gives us the system of equations: ∂f/∂x = λ∂g/∂x, ∂f/∂y = λ∂g/∂y, and g(x,y) = c.",
    "geometric_interpretation": "At the optimal point, the gradient of the objective function is parallel to the gradient of the constraint function. This means the level curve of the objective function is tangent to the constraint curve at the optimal point.",
