    ],
    "difficulties": [
      "Understanding the LIFO (Last In, First Out) nature of the call stack and how values are returned and unwound.",
      "Grasping the 'Leap of Faith' concept—trusting that the recursive step will return the correct value without needing to mentally trace every single nested call.",
      "Shifting mental models from iterative thinking (loops) to recursive thinking (breaking problems into self-similar sub-problems)."
    ],
    "classroom_activity_design": [
      {
        "phase": "1. Introduction & Hook (10 mins)",
        "activity_name": "The Matryoshka Doll Analogy",
        "description": "Bring a set of Russian nesting dolls (Matryoshka dolls) to class or display a video. Ask a student to open them. Explain that to find the smallest, solid doll (the base case), the process is always the same: open the current doll, and repeat the process with the smaller doll inside (the recursive step). Connect this physical analogy to the concept of a function calling itself."
      },
      {
        "phase": "2. Concept Exploration (15 mins)",
        "activity_name": "Deconstructing the Countdown",
        "description": "Display the pseudocode for a `countdown(n)` function on the board. Ask students to identify the line where the function calls itself (recursive case) and the line where it stops (base case). Walk through the execution step-by-step, drawing a vertical 'Call Stack' diagram on the board. Show how each call pauses, waits for the next call to finish, and then resumes, visually demonstrating the 'unwinding' phase."
      },
      {
        "phase": "3. Hands-on Practice (15 mins)",
        "activity_name": "Pair Programming: The Factorial Function",
        "description": "Challenge students in pairs to write a recursive function to calculate the factorial of a number (n!). Provide the mathematical definition: n! = n * (n-1)!, and 0! = 1. Instruct them to write out the base case and recursive case in plain English before writing the code. Circulate the room to provide hints, specifically checking if they remember to use the `return` keyword in the recursive step."
      },
      {
        "phase": "4. Summary & Reflection (10 mins)",
        "activity_name": "Recursion vs. Iteration Discussion",
        "description": "Lead a class discussion comparing the recursive `factorial` function with a standard `for` loop version. Ask students: 'Which is easier to read?' and 'Which do you think uses more memory, and why?' Conclude by summarizing the 'Leap of Faith'—the idea that when writing recursion, you assume the smaller function call works correctly without tracing it all the way down."
      }
    ],
    "teaching_aids": [
      "Projector/Smartboard for code demonstration and call stack visualization",
      "Whiteboard/Chalkboard for drawing stack frames",
